    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d399">

                <pb facs="0561" n="481" xml:id="pg481"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.C1112/83: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-12-10T13:00:00+00:00">December 10,
                            1936—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 10—9:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>604. Department’s 437, December 5, 3 p.m.<anchor n="49" xml:id="d399-f1"/>
                    Foreign Office states in view of Sir Samuel Hoare’s original suggestion at
                        Geneva,<anchor n="50" xml:id="d399-f2"/> the British are godfather to the
                    idea of such a committee but they do not intend to approach any governments
                    except through League channels. Discussions as to the formation of this
                    committee will come up before the Council meeting in January. The British
                    Government’s idea is that the committee will be one of technical inquiry very
                    much as the recent Clearings Committee.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d399-f3"/> It also
                    remains to be decided whether the invitations will be issued to nonmember states
                    by the Council or by the committee when it is set up. The British Government are
                    inclined to favor the latter proposal.</p>
                <p>The British Government likewise have the impression that the Japanese Government
                    may accept. They have no information as to the attitude of the Italian
                    Government.</p>
                <p>Foreign Office particularly referred to the fact that the terms of reference
                    specifically omit territorial questions and in view of Schacht’s<anchor n="52"
                        xml:id="d399-f4"/> speech of yesterday consider the German attitude as
                    extremely doubtful.</p>
                <p>Copy to Geneva.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d399-f1" n="49" xml:id="d399-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it requested the Ambassador to report any information he was able to
                    obtain concerning the attitude of the British Government toward the proposed
                    committee on raw materials (500.C1112/80).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d399-f2" n="50" xml:id="d399-f2-content">
                <p>The need for an inquiry into the problem of access to raw materials was suggested
                    on September 11, 1935, by Sir Samuel Hoare, British Secretary of State for
                    Foreign Affairs, in a speech before the Assembly of the League of Nations; for
                    text, see League of Nations, <hi rendition="#italic">Official Journal</hi>,
                    Special Supplement No. 138, pp. 45–46.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d399-f3" n="51" xml:id="d399-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="frus1935v01#pg_529">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1935, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 529</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d399-f4" n="52" xml:id="d399-f4-content">
                <p>Hjalmar Schacht, German Minister for Economic Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
